---
/**
 * foodcraft / FaqBM — "FAQ" (Tahanan Cafe / BM).
 *
 * ROLE: the design's FAQ element — a narrow head column (the underlined eyebrow
 * plus one Libre Caslon Text headline) beside a wide stack of hairline-separated
 * question-and-answer rows, on the page's plain WHITE ground. NO ACCORDION: the
 * design draws every answer open, and on a café page the answers ARE the
 * practicalities. Someone standing on the pavement deciding whether to come in
 * should not have to click to find out.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every value below is lifted verbatim from the
 * element carrying data-component="FAQ" in "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html",
 * mapped hex → wrapper token (R1 — not one literal hex appears in this file):
 *   section   id="faq" ............................... kept verbatim
 *   grid      minmax(0,.68fr) / minmax(0,1.32fr), gap 52px, align-items:start
 *   eyebrow   10px / .34em / 700 / #7A4E2D on a 1px #E0D3C6 underline with 9px
 *             of padding ......... THE SIGNATURE, and it is the shared .kick.
 *             It is NOT restated here — see the note under EDITABLE HOOKS.
 *   headline  Libre Caslon Text 400 clamp(27px,3.5vw,46px) / 1.14,
 *             margin .26em 0 0, #241B14 .............. var(--ink)
 *   row       padding 22px 0, border-bottom 1px #EDE6DF ....... var(--line)
 *   question  Libre Caslon Text 400 20px, margin 0 0 9px, #241B14 . var(--ink)
 *             The design declares NO line-height on this h3 and its stylesheet
 *             sets none, so `normal` is what it actually renders; it is restated
 *             below to hold off PageBM's heading default of 1.14.
 *   answer    15px / 1.82 / #5C5147, margin 0, max-width 62ch ... --ink-soft
 * The walnut --accent is the one token the design itself exposes as configurable
 * (its own data-props seeds a colour picker at #7A4E2D), so an admin colour pick
 * recolours the eyebrow and leaves the white, the ink and the hairlines alone.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — all four are declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under section id 'faq'. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook that no
 * sidebar input owns, so nothing outside this list is bound:
 *   faq.tag              — the eyebrow. Rendered as a BARE .kick with no extra
 *                          typographic declarations: the underline that is this
 *                          template's single most recognisable mark lives on the
 *                          primitive, and a font-size or border restated here
 *                          would drop it.
 *   faq.headline         — the h2, rendered with set:html so an owner can place
 *                          the template's ITALIC WALNUT EMPHASIS anywhere inside
 *                          their own sentence. That <em> rule is GLOBAL in
 *                          PageBM on purpose: scoped styles cannot reach markup
 *                          created by set:html (R3), so an italic rule written
 *                          in this file would never apply to it.
 *   faq.items.${i}.q     — the question
 *   faq.items.${i}.a     — the answer, also set:html so an owner can emphasise,
 *                          bold or link inside their own sentence
 *
 * DERIVED, and therefore carrying no data-field: only the reveal wrappers and
 * the single data-delay step on the answer column.
 * This section renders no counter, no numeral, no icon, no badge, no chip and no
 * price. The design gives the FAQ no ornament at all — the hairline between rows
 * is the only mark on it — so there is no display index to print and R6's
 * decorative-numeral half has nothing here to apply to. R6's other half DOES
 * apply, below.
 *
 * R2 — NOT ONE WORD IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE, and on an FAQ that rule is
 * load-bearing beyond the usual: an answer is a promise about a business this
 * template has never visited. So there is no opening or closing time, no "the
 * kitchen stops half an hour before close", no wifi claim, no power-outlet
 * claim, no parking, no pet / laptop / large-group policy, no minimum spend, no
 * card-versus-cash line, no "we reply within…", and no example question. The
 * design's own demo strings ("Good to know", "Often asked" and its six sample
 * rows) are the DESIGNER filling in their own template; they are not this file's
 * to type. Note that lib/derive-content-defaults.ts happens to emit that exact
 * 'Good to know' tag on every site — which is precisely why the gate below never
 * counts it.
 *
 * R5 — THE SECTION GATE COUNTS THE ROWS, never the tag and never the headline.
 * derive-content-defaults fills faq.tag ('Good to know') and faq.headline
 * ('Questions, answered') on EVERY site while emitting items only when the
 * submission actually answered something. A gate on tag||headline would ship a
 * 46px headline standing over an empty column on any café whose owner typed no
 * questions. With no filled row the whole <section> is omitted — no eyebrow, no
 * headline, no grid, no padding and no hairline.
 *
 * R6 — blank rows are dropped, but every kept row carries its ORIGINAL index
 * into data-field, so deleting question 2 can never misdirect an edit into
 * question 3.
 *
 * ROW GUARDS: a row is kept when EITHER field has content, and the two tags are
 * guarded independently. Silently vanishing an answer an owner typed is a worse
 * failure than printing a lone paragraph between two hairlines. Every guard sits
 * OUTSIDE its tag, so a half-filled row prints only what was actually typed —
 * never an empty <h3> holding open 9px of margin over a blank line box, and
 * never a hairline under a row that is not there. Fields are TRIMMED before they
 * are tested, because a value holding only a space is truthy and would print
 * exactly that empty element.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of it reads as an oversight:
 *   · NO STAR ROW and no rating ornament of any kind. Five stars printed over a
 *     customer who gave no rating is a fabricated claim; that treatment belongs
 *     to TESTIMONIALS, where it is drawn only from a numeric
 *     testimonials.items.N.rating, clamped 1–5, aria-hidden, with the number
 *     itself in the accessible label. An FAQ row carries no rating field to draw
 *     from, so there is nothing here to draw — not even the plain rule that
 *     stands in for a missing one.
 *   · no <details>/<summary> accordion. The design shows every answer open, and
 *     collapsing them would hide the practicalities behind a click.
 *   · no sticky head column. The design draws a plain `align-items:start` grid
 *     and pins nothing.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER, and that is a decision rather than an
 *     omission (R7). The helper exists to rewrite an owner's href onto the ids
 *     this page really has — for BM that set is `menu about gallery visit faq
 *     main`, with location/contact → visit, services/rooms → menu, host/why →
 *     about, work → gallery, book → main, and tel: / mailto: / http passing
 *     through untouched. This section emits NO href of any kind: no button, no
 *     link, no data-href-field, because the design gives the FAQ no action. A
 *     remap helper with nothing to remap is dead code a later reader would have
 *     to prove unused, so it is not carried. The one anchor this file owns is
 *     its own id="faq" — already in that set, and what HeaderBM's nav links to.
 *   · no button. The contact action lives in the header, the CTA band and the
 *     footer, exactly as the design places it.
 *   · no `band-dark`. The design keeps the FAQ on the white ground; the ONLY
 *     dark ground in this template is the closing CTA band, and a second one
 *     here would change the page's rhythm, not just this section's colour.
 *   · no data-block marker: editorBridge only needs one where two content
 *     prefixes share a <section>. This band is faq.* alone, so the Blocks toggle
 *     resolves to it by hook weight already — and one prefix per section is what
 *     stops a neighbouring toggle reaching in here.
 *   · no role="img" and no aria-hidden anywhere (R9). This band renders no
 *     image, no .plate and no decorative glyph, so there is no element to name
 *     and none to hide. Nothing in this file emits a conditional accessibility
 *     attribute, so there is no way for a literal one to be duplicated beside a
 *     conditional one.
 *
 * VERTICAL RHYTHM is the wrapper's, deliberately. The design writes
 * `padding:84px 30px 0` here — a BARE BOTTOM — and then opens the CTA band with
 * `margin-top:84px`, so 84px of white sits between the last hairline and the
 * espresso edge. PageBM's shared `section { padding: clamp(52px,7vw,84px) 0 }`
 * puts that same 84px on THIS section's bottom instead, which is why the CTA
 * band drops the design's `margin-top`. The dependency runs both ways: a
 * `padding-bottom:0` here would close the gap to nothing, and a margin there on
 * top of this padding would open it to ~168px. Neither file may move alone.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE MEASURE is left to the wrapper's `.h2 max-width:24ch`. The design
 * sets none, and at desktop 24ch is never the constraint anyway: the .68fr track
 * is ~377px inside the 1160px wrap, far narrower than 24 characters of 46px
 * Libre Caslon Text. Once the grid folds at 900px the column goes full width and
 * the wrapper's own 760px pass releases the measure, which is the behaviour a
 * long owner headline wants. The one .h2 declaration this file DOES override is
 * the size — see the clamp ladder in the style block.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style>, no is:global. Both the headline and the answers are
 * injected with set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach inside them: the
 * headline's italic walnut <em> is styled by PageBM's global :where(.h2 em), and
 * an <em>, <strong> or <a> inside an answer is reached from here via :global().
 *
 * VERIFIED CONTRAST on the white ground (the design's own palette, as measured
 * in PageBM). Nothing in this section is below AA:
 *   eyebrow --accent/--paper 7.06 · question --ink/--paper 14.9
 *   answer --ink-soft/--paper 7.29 · an owner's link --accent/--paper 7.06
 * The weakest token in the palette, --muted at 5.71, is not used here at all.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBM passes it to every section uniformly.
// This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// The schema declares 'faq.items' with fallbackPaths ['faq'], so the stored
// value is legitimately EITHER {tag, headline, items:[…]} or a bare array.
// Both shapes are read.
const faq = content.faq || {};
const tag = (faq.tag || '').trim();
const headline = (faq.headline || '').trim();

// Legacy submissions spell the pair {question, answer}; the schema (and every
// current writer) spells {q, a}. Read both, but the data-field hook always
// spells q / a — that is the path the sidebar input owns.
type Item = { q?: string; a?: string; question?: string; answer?: string };
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(faq.items)
  ? faq.items
  : (Array.isArray(faq) ? faq : []);
// Optional chaining, not a bare read: a stored list can carry a null slot (a row
// deleted by an older editor left a hole), and reading `.q` off it would throw
// at BUILD time and take the whole site down, not just this band.
const qOf = (it: Item): string => it?.q ?? it?.question ?? '';
const aOf = (it: Item): string => it?.a ?? it?.answer ?? '';

// R6 — carry the ORIGINAL index past the blank filter, so removing row 2 cannot
// misdirect an edit into row 3.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ i, q: qOf(it).trim(), a: aOf(it).trim() }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.q || r.a));

// With neither eyebrow nor headline the head column would spend a whole grid
// track on nothing, so it is omitted and the grid collapses to a single column.
const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="tc-faq" id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class:list={['tc-faq-grid', !hasHead && 'tc-faq-grid--solo']}>
        {hasHead && (
          <div class="tc-faq-head reveal">
            {tag && <span class="kick tc-faq-kick" data-field="faq.tag">{tag}</span>}
            {headline && <h2 class="h2 tc-faq-h" data-field="faq.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
          </div>
        )}
        <div class="tc-faq-list reveal" data-delay="1">
          {rows.map((row) => (
            <div class="tc-faq-row">
              {row.q && <h3 class="tc-faq-q" data-field={`faq.items.${row.i}.q`}>{row.q}</h3>}
              {row.a && <p class="tc-faq-a" data-field={`faq.items.${row.i}.a`} set:html={row.a}></p>}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The section keeps PageBM's shared padding and the page's white ground: the
     design puts no band under the FAQ. min-width:0 so a long unbroken word
     inside a row can never widen the grid and push the page sideways (R8). */
  .tc-faq { min-width: 0; }

  /* The design's split, verbatim: a narrow head against a wide answer column.
     minmax(0,…) on BOTH tracks is what lets a row shrink instead of overflowing
     its track — an `auto` minimum would let one long word set the column width. */
  .tc-faq-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .68fr) minmax(0, 1.32fr);
    gap: 52px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* Head suppressed: one track, so the rows take the full wrap rather than
     leaving a .68fr column of white beside them. */
  .tc-faq-grid--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  .tc-faq-head { min-width: 0; }
  /* The eyebrow's type, colour, border-bottom and 9px of padding are the shared
     .kick and are NOT restated — the underline is this design's single most
     recognisable mark and a redeclaration here would drop it. Only the wrapping
     behaviour is touched, because the head is the NARROW track (.68fr) and runs
     out of room first: a derived tag longer than ~377px would otherwise sit
     outside its column, and the wrapper only relaxes strings below 760px while
     this grid is still two columns down to 900px. */
  .tc-faq-kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: clamp(27px,3.5vw,46px) / 1.14 / margin .26em 0 0 — a step smaller
     than the shared .h2's clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px), and the margin runs ABOVE the
     headline rather than below it, because nothing sits under it in this column.
     Line-height, face, weight and colour are already the .h2 primitive's, so
     only the two divergences are written.

     THE CLAMP LADDER, checked ACROSS BREAKPOINTS because an audit on the
     previous template found a headline that GREW as the viewport narrowed. This
     rule outranks the wrapper's :where(.h2) at every width, its 760px pass
     included, so the whole ramp is this file's to keep monotonic:
       1314px+ .. 46px (cap)   ·   1160px .. 40.6px   ·   900px .. 31.5px
       771px and below .. 27px (floor), and it holds flat from there
     No media query in this file touches this size — see the note in the 520
     block — so that ramp is the whole of it: font-size here only ever
     DECREASES as the viewport narrows, and it never goes under the design's
     own floor. */
  .tc-faq-h {
    font-size: clamp(27px, 3.5vw, 46px);
    margin: .26em 0 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .tc-faq-list { min-width: 0; }

  /* Every row carries the hairline, the last one included — that is how the
     design closes the stack against the espresso CTA band beneath it. */
  .tc-faq-row {
    padding: 22px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* PageBM sets :where(h1,h2,h3,h4) to Libre Caslon Text 400 --ink at
     line-height 1.14. The face, weight and colour are the design's already; only
     the leading is restated, and it is restated as `normal` because that is
     literally what the design renders — its h3 carries no line-height and its
     stylesheet sets none. Guessing a number would be a divergence, and the
     wrapper's 1.14 would be a VISIBLE one the moment an owner's question runs
     longer than the designer's and wraps: at 1.14 the descenders of the first
     line crowd the caps of the second. */
  .tc-faq-q {
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: normal;
    margin: 0 0 9px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* PageBM's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1); qualifying with the row
     class puts this comfortably above it so the design's zero margin actually
     lands. The answer is deliberately NOT the wrapper's .body primitive — .body
     is a clamped 14.5–15px at 1.8, and the design pins this run at a flat 15px
     at 1.82. */
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.82;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 62ch;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* The answer arrives through set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach inside it
     (R3). An owner's emphasis keeps the answer's own colour: walnut on a 15px
     run inside a paragraph reads as a link that does not work — this template's
     italic walnut is the HEADLINE's signature and stays there. */
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a :global(em),
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a :global(i) { font-style: italic; color: inherit; }
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a :global(strong),
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a :global(b) { font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  /* An owner who pastes a link into an answer gets the walnut, underlined on
     hover only — the same restraint as the wrapper's .linkc and the same reason:
     a colour that does not move mid-interaction cannot fail a contrast check
     halfway through one. */
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a :global(a) {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a :global(a:hover) {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-bottom-color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* ── 900 · this section's OWN grid, which is the part R8 leaves to this file.
        The head column narrows past legibility well before the answers do, so
        the two tracks fold to one and the headline sits above its own stack.
        No font-size is touched here: the base clamp is already handing over
        31.5px at this width and any cap written below that number would make
        the headline JUMP as the viewport crossed 900px. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .tc-faq-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 26px; }
  }

  /* ── 520 · nothing may push the page sideways. Both tracks are already
        minmax(0,…) and every string in here breaks anywhere, so the remaining
        work is measure and scale. The 62ch cap comes off so an answer is not
        set narrower than the phone it is being read on.

        THE HEADLINE IS NOT RE-CLAMPED HERE. Its own clamp reaches the design's
        27px FLOOR at 771px and holds it flat from there down — that floor is
        the design's own answer for a narrow viewport, and going under it would
        be a divergence with nothing asking for it. Re-stating a clamp inside a
        max-width query is also the exact construct that shipped a headline
        which GREW as the viewport narrowed on the previous template, which is
        why CredentialsBM and LocationBM record the same decision for their own
        smaller-clamp headlines. Nothing here needs the extra step: 27px of
        Libre Caslon sets "Questions, answered" in ~241px, inside the 294px wrap
        of a 320px phone, and overflow-wrap:anywhere covers a longer owner
        headline. The measure is handled too — the wrapper's 760 pass already
        supplies .h2 max-width:none and this file overrides no max-width, so a
        restatement here would only be dead weight. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .tc-faq-row { padding: 20px 0; }
    .tc-faq-q { font-size: 19px; }
    .tc-faq-row .tc-faq-a { font-size: 14.5px; max-width: none; }
  }
</style>
